  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 330 AM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nThe start of the Long Term period shall be marked by an active \nweather pattern. Tomorrow evening into Wednesday night, an upper \nlevel low translates over the Great Basin toward the Rockies. \nMeanwhile, the upper ridge responsible for recent above normal \ntemperatures drifts eastward over the southeastern CONUS. Rich \nmoisture from what is currently Hurricane Simon will be entrained \nwithin southwesterly flow aloft. At the surface, a trough looks to \nextend from the western Texas Panhandle down into the Big Bend \ntomorrow evening, drawing Gulf moisture toward our region. As such, \nensembles suggest PWAT values between 1.25-1.8\" over far SE NM, the \nPermian Basin, the Trans Pecos, the Davis Mountains, and the Big \nBend tomorrow evening and overnight. Provided this materializes, we \nwill have to monitor for the potential of heavy rainfall and a \nsubsequent flash flood threat. CAMs depict nearly areawide rounds of \nscattered to numerous (30-70% chance) showers/storms headed from \nsouthwest to northeast through tomorrow night. Aside from heavy \nrainfall, CAPE values of 1000-1500 J/kg, bulk shear values of 25-35 \nkts, and mid-level lapse rates of 6-7 C/km will support a couple of \nstrong to severe storms tomorrow night into early Wednesday morning. \nThe primary threats with the strongest storms, outside of flash \nflooding, will be small hail and damaging winds.\n\nA similar set up looks to play out Wednesday and Wednesday night, as \nthe upper low gradually opens up and lifts over the Rockies into the \nCentral and Northern Plains. The best rain chances shift slightly \neastward compared to Tuesday, though still encompass much of the \nsame areas. Again, rounds of scattered to numerous (30-70% chance) \nshowers/storms are forecast over the Big Bend, the Trans Pecos, far \nSE NM, and the Permian Basin Wednesday into Wednesday night. With \nPWAT values remaining roughly similar to Tuesday's, instances of \nflash flooding will again be a hazard to monitor during this time \nframe. The potential for severe storm development will focus more \nover the Permian Basin, the Trans Pecos, and far SE NM Wednesday \nafternoon and evening. In these areas, a couple of storms will be \ncapable of producing damaging winds and small hail. Otherwise, \ntemperatures Wednesday afternoon shall largely top out in the 80s,\nwith 90s near and along the Rio Grande.\n\nThursday, the shortwave upper trough lifts over the Central Plains \nand heads east, while a longwave trough resides over the Desert \nSouthwest. Deep moisture will continue within southwesterly flow \naloft, while a cold front slowly drops down across the Plains. The \ncold front itself is expected to sweep through our area Thursday \nnight into Friday. However, ahead of the front, disturbances aloft \ncombines with deep moisture, promoting additional scattered to \nnumerous shower and storm development. The potential for heavy \nrainfall and resultant flash flooding persists Thursday into \nThursday night, especially along the frontal boundary. Rain chances \nbegin to taper down from east to west Friday into Friday night, as a \ncooler and drier air mass is ushered in behind the cold front. Highs \nFriday and Saturday are forecast to reach the 60s for most \nlocations, with 50s in and near the higher terrain. Lows Friday \nnight and Saturday night generally dip into the 40s. Highs then \nrebound into the 60s and 70s Sunday afternoon.",
